-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions: independent edits from the same starting point stay intact as competing revisions for a coordinator to resolve | Field-aware three-way merge: tablets send a base snapshot and structured changes; disjoint field changes combine automatically and only overlapping changes go to review | Automatic merging needs maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and those rules can combine scientifically related edits (a species identification and its cover estimate) without anyone noticing. A larger review queue is the accepted cost: about 6,000 visits a season and two coordinators make it bearable, and it keeps every exported value either one worker's whole submission or a coordinator's explicit resolution. Merging would cut review volume and get more visits into each Friday export. |
+| Revision identity | Client-generated revision UUID, doubling as the idempotency key | Server-assigned revision IDs with a separate idempotency key | A tablet offline for 48 hours must be able to base a second edit on a first it has not submitted; a server-assigned ID does not exist yet at that point. One identifier instead of two removes a class of mismatch. |
+| Late edit on a superseded base | Accept it as a competing revision | Reject it and require the worker to redo the edit on the current revision | Rejection discards a submitted edit unless the worker re-enters it, and treats arrival order as authority. |
+| What an unresolved visit returns as current | Nothing; only the competing revisions | Return the shared base, or the earliest-received competing revision, as a provisional current value | Any provisional value is one a coordinator has not chosen and could reach the partner CSV as if reviewed. |
+| Scope of a resolution | Must name every present competing revision | Allow resolving a subset, leaving the rest competing | A subset resolution yields another unresolved state with a new competing revision, lengthening review without settling the visit; naming every one also guarantees no competing revision is superseded unseen. |
+| Identical competing payloads | Still competing; a coordinator resolves them | Settle automatically when all competing payloads are identical | The record store applies one rule with no payload comparison. Review-export can show that the payloads match so the resolution is a single confirmation. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Withdrawing or deleting a visit entered in error. The contract has no delete; a visit can only be revised.
